WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:08.980
SIERRA: It’s March, and it’s been nearly
a year since COVID-19 was declared a pandemic.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:13.840
There’s no two ways about it, it’s a bitter
anniversary, but it’s also a time to think

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.850
about how far we’ve come.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:19.359
There was so much confusion at the start about
how to protect ourselves.

00:00:19.359 --> 00:00:24.890
Now, a year later, we’ve learned a lot about
how to stay safe, and we’ve developed highly

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.480
effective vaccines in record time.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.619
We’re starting to see the light at the end
of the tunnel.

00:00:31.619 --> 00:00:36.300
But there is at least one more hurdle that
threatens to undo our progress.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.780
All around the world, wealthy countries are
hoarding vaccines, leaving huge swaths of

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.890
the global population without access.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:49.390
Global health officials say that this could
be a lethal mistake for rich and poor countries

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:50.390
alike.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:53.899
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It
Matters.

00:00:53.899 --> 00:00:58.750
Today, why a global pandemic needs a global
response.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:06.049
NBC News: It’s been almost a year ago when
the country began shutting down from Covid

00:01:06.049 --> 00:01:09.610
and for the first time, there seems to be
a real reason to have cautious optimism.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:13.830
PBS NewsHour: Who gets vaccines and when are
not only serious questions in the U.S but

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.760
also around the globe.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:23.010
DW: In the global fight against Covid-19,
its mutations have been proving to be the

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:24.460
next great challenge.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:27.360
SIERRA: So we have a lot of really important
stuff to talk about.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.280
But I feel like one of those important things
is that both you and I are South Brooklyn

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.280
children.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.680
And I feel like it was really important to
just get that out of the way.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.610
FAUCI: Where were you?

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:38.630
Where were you in South Brooklyn?

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.189
SIERRA: I'm from Manhattan Beach.

00:01:40.189 --> 00:01:41.189
FAUCI: You are?

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:42.189
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:01:42.189 --> 00:01:43.189
FAUCI: I'm from Bensonhurst.

00:01:43.189 --> 00:01:44.189
SIERRA: I know.

00:01:44.189 --> 00:01:45.189
Yeah.

00:01:45.189 --> 00:01:48.470
Well, I you need no introduction, but I'll
just start the way we always start, which

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:49.960
is what is your name?

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:50.960
And what do you do?

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.229
FAUCI: My name is Dr. Anthony Fauci.

00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:58.299
And I'm the director of the National Institute
of Allergy and Infectious Diseases at the

00:01:58.299 --> 00:02:00.000
National Institutes of Health.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.000
SIERRA: Alright.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.570
So, let’s say that the majority of Americans
were vaccinated tomorrow.

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.840
Would we be fully in the clear?

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:13.760
FAUCI: So if we got let's say 70 to 85 percent
of the people in the country vaccinated, then

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.129
we would be very much better off than we are
now.

00:02:17.129 --> 00:02:20.580
But there's a real catch to your question.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:27.180
Because even if we got the overwhelming majority
of the population vaccinated, there is a big

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.050
wide world out there.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.700
And that's the global pandemic.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:39.890
So if the rest of the world did not get COVID-19,
or SARS-CoV-2, under control, there would

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:47.010
always be a looming threat over us that the
virus would be mutating, would be changing,

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:53.599
and we would get new variants that even if
we felt ourselves protected in this country,

00:02:53.599 --> 00:03:00.310
sooner or later, a variant or a mutation could
come here, and essentially circumvent the

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.560
protection of the vaccine.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.049
So I think when you think in terms of a pandemic,
you have to remember it is global.

00:03:07.049 --> 00:03:14.470
And it requires a global response, not just
a single country response.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:18.770
HAASS: In the old days, when we used to get
on airplanes, you remember that?

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:20.129
When we could all travel?

00:03:20.129 --> 00:03:25.060
One of the first things you get is this disembodied
voice that would say, in the event of a loss

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:29.160
of cabin pressure, oxygen masks will come
down from the overhead.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:34.439
SIERRA: This is Richard Haass, president of
the Council on Foreign Relations, former director

00:03:34.439 --> 00:03:40.440
of policy planning for the State Department,
adviser to two Presidents, and...my boss.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:44.370
HAASS: And you are instructed by this voice
to put it on yourself.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:49.500
And then only after you yourself are safe
and taken care of, then you turn to your children,

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:50.920
your neighbors and others.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:52.680
And that's our instinct here.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.000
We've got to jettison that instinct.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.261
Unless the rest of the world gets vaccinated,
it doesn't just threaten their lives, that

00:03:59.261 --> 00:04:00.670
threatens ours too.

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:05.049
Because if we don't take care of others, variants
will continue to break out.

00:04:05.049 --> 00:04:10.080
And when variants break out over there, they
will figure out a way to come here.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.110
So then we end up in a race that never quite
ends, and that we never quite win.

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:19.000
SIERRA: To stay ahead of the curve we have
to understand variants.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:22.919
And in order to understand variants, we have
to understand mutations.

00:04:22.919 --> 00:04:27.139
And in order to understand mutations, we went
to the country’s medical explainer-in-chief.

00:04:27.139 --> 00:04:30.810
SIERRA: So what is a mutation?

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:32.210
Where do they tend to come from?

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:34.180
Why do we need to worry about them?

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:39.789
FAUCI: When viruses replicate, which they
do very, very rapidly, particularly when you

00:04:39.789 --> 00:04:45.750
have literally 100 million people infected,
which is what we have right now in the world

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:49.250
with, you know, over 2.4 million deaths.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:56.160
When you have that amount of replication,
as the virus replicates, it makes mistakes

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.000
in reproducing itself.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.740
That's called a mutation.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:04.150
SIERRA: Mutations are alterations to a sequence.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:07.350
A lot of people explain this by comparing
it to a misspelled word.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:09.610
So, let’s say you’re sending a text.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:11.979
You’re moving fast, and you spell something
wrong.

00:05:11.979 --> 00:05:13.320
Probably not a big deal.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.039
Your friend understands what you mean.

00:05:15.039 --> 00:05:20.200
But what if that misspelled word actually
changes the meaning of your sentence?

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:22.980
Now your friend is upset, and you have to
deal with the fallout.

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:25.490
A virus mutation is similar.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:28.560
As viruses replicate quickly, mistakes are
made.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.389
Most of these mistakes won’t matter much,
but sometimes the change allows a virus to

00:05:33.389 --> 00:05:35.580
become something very different.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:43.960
FAUCI: When you get a constellation of mutations,
then you can get what's called a variant,

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:52.229
namely, a strain of virus that varies considerably
from what the original virus was.

00:05:52.229 --> 00:05:53.950
That's very important.

00:05:53.950 --> 00:06:02.020
Because if you make a vaccine against one
of the iterations of the virus, and it changes,

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:10.150
mutates, and becomes a variant, you may either
weaken or even lose the protection of the

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:11.150
vaccine.

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:15.870
SIERRA: Mutations that can’t be stopped
with current vaccines are worrisome, but they

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.900
aren’t the only concern.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:22.630
Epidemiologists are also on the lookout for
strains that are more deadly, strains that

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:27.330
are more contagious, and strains that are
not detectable with our current tests.

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:33.380
No matter where they arise, variants can travel
and become a problem for the entire world.

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:37.600
And this isn’t hypothetical; new variants
that developed in California and New York

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:41.930
are spreading as we speak, as are several
that emerged abroad.

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:47.420
CBC News: The new coronavirus variant identified
in the UK has now made it into Canada.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:52.230
ABC News: News this evening of the dangerous
South African variant has now been detected

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:56.319
in a patient here in the New York City in
a hospital.

00:06:56.319 --> 00:06:58.570
KHOU 11 News: We begin with the Brazil variant
of the coronavirus detected in Minnesota.

00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:02.890
Scientists say this variant spreads more quickly.

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:11.629
FAUCI: Any place where you have a lot of viral
replication with a lot of infection is by

00:07:11.629 --> 00:07:16.819
definition a hotspot for the evolution of
mutations.

00:07:16.819 --> 00:07:26.570
It's a very firm axiom of virology, that if
you're not replicating, you can't mutate.

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:34.720
So the best way to prevent a virus from mutating
is to dampen down dramatically its spread

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:42.470
from person to person, because the more it
spreads, the more opportunity it has to mutate.

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:46.199
SIERRA: So...could this set us back to square
one?

00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:52.030
FAUCI: Well, it is unlikely that it would
set us back to square one, but it certainly

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:53.490
could interfere with the progress.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:57.199
Let me give you the scientific explanation
for that.

00:07:57.199 --> 00:08:02.620
So right now, there are two major variants
that we are concerned about.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:08.870
One of them is a variant that evolved from
mutations that occurred in the strain in the

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:09.880
UK.

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:12.889
And it is now dominated in the UK.

00:08:12.889 --> 00:08:18.460
It transmits from person to person more efficiently
than the original virus.

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:24.710
And it is even more dangerous, that it could
potentially make you more ill and even lead

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:28.220
to a greater degree of hospitalization.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:31.590
Right now, that variant is in the United States.

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:37.250
And modelers projected that by the time you
get to the end of March, that it could be

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:41.190
the dominant strain in the United States.

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:42.469
That's sobering news.

00:08:42.469 --> 00:08:47.459
The good news is that the vaccines that we
are distributing now in the United States

00:08:47.459 --> 00:08:51.300
happen to work very well against [it].

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:57.320
However, there's another troublesome variant
that is dominating in the Republic of South

00:08:57.320 --> 00:08:58.870
Africa.

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:05.010
That one is replicating rapidly and causing
a lot of disease in South Africa.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:10.170
It is also in the United States, but in a
very minor way.

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:17.130
The reason we're more concerned is that the
vaccines that we're using are diminished multiple

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:20.290
folds in their capability of controlling that.

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:23.870
It hasn't eliminated the ability to control
it.

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:28.320
But it considerably diminishes its capability.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:32.440
So that's the one that we really need to watch
out for.

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:37.519
SIERRA: As the virus replicates scientists
know that it’s only a matter of time until

00:09:37.519 --> 00:09:39.510
other variants emerge.

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:44.399
And the possibility is always there that a
new variant could be more deadly, harder to

00:09:44.399 --> 00:09:48.529
trace, and perhaps even immune to existing
vaccines.

00:09:48.529 --> 00:09:52.620
This is why vaccinating your own country isn’t
the real finish line.

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:59.560
FAUCI: The entire global community needs to
have access to vaccines, if we really are

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:06.940
going to durably control this outbreak, which
brings us to the point of the fact that the

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:15.130
United States and other developed nations,
like the UK, and the EU, and Canada, and Australia,

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:22.330
and Japan, and other countries that have resources,
very well would likely have to put in whatever

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:29.459
it takes to get that part of the world that
doesn't have the resources to develop and

00:10:29.459 --> 00:10:30.710
or administer vaccines.

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:37.660
So in other words, it's got to be a global
effort that is involving countries that have

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:43.010
the resources to be able to contribute to
the overall effort.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:48.850
SIERRA: The process for obtaining vaccines
is complicated.

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:52.790
Most of it has been done through a bidding
process in which wealthy countries competed

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:58.339
with each other to secure as many doses as
possible directly from manufacturers.

00:10:58.339 --> 00:11:02.580
This distribution model is based on money,
rather than population or need.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:07.200
As a result, a small number of nations were
able to secure a majority of doses.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:11.420
CBS News: A few rich countries, including
U.S. and Canada, have 14 percent of the world

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:15.420
population but control 53 percent of the vaccine
supply.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:20.800
CNA: 40 percent of the world would not have
widespread access to vaccines until 2023.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.140
That’s more than eighty five countries.

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:29.790
CNN: The basic problem is in how the vaccine
is distributed around the world, not based

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:33.060
on whether there is most need, but the most
money.

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:37.380
SIERRA: There is a global effort to share
and distribute vaccines more equitably.

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:39.120
It’s called COVAX.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:46.170
It’s a joint effort of three international
organizations, the WHO, GAVI, and CEPI, and

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:51.920
it aims to provide at least two billion vaccine
doses to the world’s most vulnerable populations

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:53.790
by the end of 2021.

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:56.950
It’s already begun sending doses to West
Africa.

00:11:56.950 --> 00:11:59.220
But COVAX is only a start.

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:04.310
It’s not clear if it will meet its fundraising
goals, and even then, COVAX will only be equipped

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:09.620
to deliver vaccines to the world’s most
vulnerable people over a period of years.

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:14.839
That’s nowhere near enough to achieve herd
immunity globally and end the pandemic.

00:12:14.839 --> 00:12:19.220
Without increased production capacity and
the donation of actual doses, it’s going

00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:22.630
to be years before poor countries get vaccinated.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:26.269
And that means years of gambling on new variants.

00:12:26.269 --> 00:12:30.510
In order to better understand how things are
playing out in the developing world we talked

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:36.070
to Tidjane Thiam, a former CEO of Credit Suisse,
who has been appointed by the African Union

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:40.900
to solicit international support for the bloc’s
fight against COVID.

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:47.040
SIERRA: So how might things play out if Africa
doesn't receive international support in this

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:48.220
battle against COVID?

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:49.779
THIAM: Oh, it would be terrible.

00:12:49.779 --> 00:12:50.899
Of course.

00:12:50.899 --> 00:12:54.389
We've all said many times, but nobody's safe
until everybody's safe.

00:12:54.389 --> 00:12:59.950
I mean that is absolutely the case for something
like for Coronavirus, as contagious as COVID

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:00.950
is.

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:03.699
And really Africa had been doing well, going
into this crisis.

00:13:03.699 --> 00:13:08.660
The last twenty years have been a period of
huge growth and progress for Africa.

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:13.410
And we feel that there's a real risk that
Africa falls back by ten or fifteen years

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:18.250
in terms of its development, and for a part
of the world where you have 1.2 billion people

00:13:18.250 --> 00:13:19.500
that would be catastrophic.

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:25.089
SIERRA: So how did African nations fare in
the race to secure vaccine doses?

00:13:25.089 --> 00:13:26.720
THIAM: Well this race is ongoing.

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:28.950
A number of initiatives have been taken.

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:31.490
The most visible is COVAX.

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:35.230
And the objective of COVAX is to cover about
20 percent of the population.

00:13:35.230 --> 00:13:40.470
And you know that the key number to achieve
herd immunity is about 60 percent.

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:42.090
So we still have a big gap there.

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:47.300
You know, Africa has honestly reacted reasonably
well, the heads of states are very involved.

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:49.260
The African Union has played a key role.

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:53.209
But one thing, the health systems are very
weak.

00:13:53.209 --> 00:13:57.660
The second wave, which we are in right now
has been much worse than the first one.

00:13:57.660 --> 00:14:01.009
And you are starting to see the health systems
cracking at the seams.

00:14:01.009 --> 00:14:06.690
So for the same level of contagion, you are
likely to see a much higher level of mortality

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:09.560
in Africa, which would be really tragic.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:14.769
And we don't want to renew the experience
we had with HIV AIDS, where for ten years

00:14:14.769 --> 00:14:21.740
we were not able to provide treatment to Africa,
and people died by the hundreds of thousands

00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:23.850
when treatments were available.

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:30.050
SIERRA: So are there other important ways
that countries like the US can support the

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:33.950
battle against COVID in Africa beyond providing
the doses themselves?

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:37.700
THIAM: I think the first issue is resources,
money.

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:45.500
We have worked hard to have a kind of debt
deal with the G20 to create some fiscal space

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:51.380
for the African government so they can spend
their scarce resources on procuring for vaccines

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:52.380
and paying back with it.

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:54.380
So this is an ongoing process.

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:59.190
And there are negotiations underway, country
by country, including for the first time China

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:01.650
as a creditor and also the private sector.

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:08.500
So if we get that done, then there will be
enough resources to vaccinate Africans to

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:11.089
a level that provides herd immunity, or 60
percent.

00:15:11.089 --> 00:15:17.470
So what we want from the U.S. is support from
that initiative and mobilization to ensure

00:15:17.470 --> 00:15:22.579
that we get a physical supply of vaccines.Plus
the delivery, which is also hampered by the

00:15:22.579 --> 00:15:26.029
weakness of health system, plus the issues
of storage.

00:15:26.029 --> 00:15:30.449
So we need help in buying the vaccines, but
we also need help in ensuring that there's

00:15:30.449 --> 00:15:34.830
a delivery system to keep the doses effective
so that people are actually protected.

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:37.769
SIERRA: So you have a lot of experience in
finance.

00:15:37.769 --> 00:15:42.390
What do you see playing out economically if
the world does not move to address rampant

00:15:42.390 --> 00:15:44.600
COVID transmission in Africa and elsewhere?

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:52.180
THIAM: I mean, Africa today is very well integrated
into the world economy and plays a big role

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:54.959
today for GDP of Africa is $2.5 trillion.

00:15:54.959 --> 00:15:57.339
It's as big as India's.

00:15:57.339 --> 00:16:02.690
So it's a very large player in international
affairs, and any significant deterioration

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:06.370
in Africa will then reverberate across the
world.

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:12.339
And you don't want a situation where we keep
fighting fires, and this virus travels around

00:16:12.339 --> 00:16:17.690
impacting region after region, because also
in the end, this will translate into political

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:22.889
instability, conflict and situation that will
impact everybody around the planet.

00:16:22.889 --> 00:16:27.600
SIERRA: Do you have any security concerns
about the countries that are fighting COVID

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:30.420
without vaccine access and without economic
support?

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:32.269
HAASS: The short answer is yes.

00:16:32.269 --> 00:16:37.630
What worries me about COVID is that in countries
that can't get the vaccine, this becomes a

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:40.769
form or source, rather, of state weakening.

00:16:40.769 --> 00:16:45.320
This becomes one of those things that will
weaken the ability of the state to perform

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:51.510
the functions we expect states to do, provide
public services, among which is security.

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:56.009
So there's a recession going on in much of
the world, this will prolong and deepen a

00:16:56.009 --> 00:17:01.079
recession, it will infect security personnel,
it will make it harder for people to go to

00:17:01.079 --> 00:17:04.280
their jobs to stay in their jobs to be in
schools.

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:05.990
This is classic of a weak state.

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:12.160
And weak states, by definition, become territories
where bad things happen, where terrorism,

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:16.280
drug cartels, pirates and others set up shop.

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:20.730
SIERRA: How are we going to find the political
will?

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:22.500
Because, even me, right?

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:27.540
I'm counting the days until my friends, my
family, especially my parents can get the

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:28.540
vaccine.

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:29.540
I want the vaccine.

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:31.539
I mean, protecting your own is a strong impulse.

00:17:31.539 --> 00:17:36.470
How do we convince people to support the delivery
of vaccines to strangers in other countries?

00:17:36.470 --> 00:17:40.789
HAASS: Look, it’s a question I ask myself,
it's totally legit.

00:17:40.789 --> 00:17:45.880
And I expect it's the reason that thus far,
the Biden administration, which wants to be

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:48.380
helpful, has limited its help to economic
help.

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:52.539
It has not been willing to make any commitment
as the French president has urged that maybe

00:17:52.539 --> 00:17:56.980
even a small percentage of vaccine produced
here to be sent overseas.

00:17:56.980 --> 00:18:03.390
SIERRA: The Biden administration has pledged
$2 billion, with another $2 billion to come,

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:04.570
to COVAX.

00:18:04.570 --> 00:18:09.429
But it has not yet committed to the more politically
sensitive idea of sending American vaccine

00:18:09.429 --> 00:18:10.470
doses abroad.

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:12.120
HAASS: It's a hard sell.

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:14.080
It's a counterintuitive case.

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:16.790
But let me suggest at least two arguments.

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:18.870
That one is the one we've been talking about.

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:21.600
It's the argument of enlightened self- interest.

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:26.030
Don't think of this as a form of just helping
others, not that there's anything wrong with

00:18:26.030 --> 00:18:27.030
that.

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:28.929
But think of this as a way of helping ourselves.

00:18:28.929 --> 00:18:33.690
The other thing we could do is ramp up supply
over estimates and say we're going to produce,

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:38.049
for argument's sake, enough for two million
doses a day here in the United States, which

00:18:38.049 --> 00:18:40.140
is a fantastic level.

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:44.200
If however, we're able to get above that,
we will make those available elsewhere.

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:48.600
Because if we can give doses at the rate of
two million a day, you can do the math, that's

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:50.250
sixty million a month.

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:53.559
That gets us to where we need to be when we
want to get there.

00:18:53.559 --> 00:18:58.170
So I think if the President could explain
that, people wouldn't think that we're taking

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:02.640
it out of our own pockets or literally out
of our own arms and putting it in the arms

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:03.640
of others.

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:08.140
SIERRA: If things continue on their current
trajectory, and the U.S. continues to meet

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:12.450
its ambitious goals for vaccinating its own
population, we need to start to think about

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:17.590
the point at which sending vaccines abroad
becomes a better way of protecting ourselves

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:20.220
than simply using them at home.

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:23.020
FAUCI: You can do both.

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:29.480
By the time we get to July, we will have the
contractual arrangements fulfilled by at least

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:36.050
two of the companies to get a total of 600
million doses, for 300 million people.

00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:40.590
SIERRA: In fact, the outlook has gotten even
better since we spoke with Dr. Fauci.

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:45.370
The Biden administration now says the U.S.
will have enough doses to cover its entire

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:47.140
population by May.

00:19:47.140 --> 00:19:54.480
FAUCI: We should not have to stop there, nor
should other developed nations, nor should

00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:59.180
other companies say we're done, we've made
enough vaccines.

00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:03.020
We're knee deep and solving our own problem
right now.

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:09.370
But that does not mean that we should not
join the rest of the world, and maybe lead

00:20:09.370 --> 00:20:17.380
the rest of the world in addressing the globality
of this pandemic, as opposed to focusing only

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:19.600
on our individual countries.

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:27.450
It means that you have to dramatically increase
the global capacity to make vaccines.

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:35.340
Because we have considerable vaccine-making
capability, but not enough for the whole world.

00:20:35.340 --> 00:20:43.159
And so what we need to do is we need to build
the capacity in the form of factories and

00:20:43.159 --> 00:20:51.059
plants that can make vaccine in the billions
and billions of doses, for the billions and

00:20:51.059 --> 00:20:54.800
billions of people who live on this planet.

00:20:54.800 --> 00:21:02.059
So that would require putting up physical
facilities, getting them fitted to make vaccines,

00:21:02.059 --> 00:21:04.850
making them in large quantities.

00:21:04.850 --> 00:21:10.700
And that is based upon the investment in resources
to do just that.

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:16.890
So what we're going to need is a global commitment,
including by “we” the United States.

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:22.630
SIERRA: You've written recently about the
United States’s diminished global reputation.

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:26.520
I mean, do you think this is an opportunity
to start earning that back?

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:27.720
HAASS: Well, absolutely.

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:31.870
This is a real chance to show that the United
States is back on the playing field, that

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:33.220
this is a country of capacity.

00:21:33.220 --> 00:21:35.590
But it’s also a country of care.

00:21:35.590 --> 00:21:40.350
It's a way of not leaving the playing field
simply to others, it gets us back in the game.

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:43.510
Look it's one of those chances to do good
and do well.

00:21:43.510 --> 00:21:46.370
You know, you don't often get twofers like
this in foreign policy.

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:48.400
But this is a twofer.

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:53.049
We can help others, we can enhance our prestige
overseas, it’s a threefer, and we can make

00:21:53.049 --> 00:21:55.320
ourselves safer at the same time.

00:21:55.320 --> 00:21:58.150
And the challenge will have less to do with
doing it.

00:21:58.150 --> 00:22:01.160
I actually think doing it in this case is
not all that hard.

00:22:01.160 --> 00:22:02.160
It's explaining it.

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:04.900
And it's not having it be a political liability.

00:22:04.900 --> 00:22:07.690
But that then is a communications challenge.

00:22:07.690 --> 00:22:09.539
That's an educational challenge.

00:22:09.539 --> 00:22:11.059
It's an explaining challenge.

00:22:11.059 --> 00:22:12.570
And I think we are up to it.

00:22:12.570 --> 00:22:14.700
SIERRA: Won’t we need help?

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:20.320
How do we get places like Japan or Canada
or the EU to do their part?

00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:24.440
HAASS: This should not be and cannot be a
U.S. only effort.

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:29.029
But in my experience, you're much more able
to talk the talk, much more persuasively,

00:22:29.029 --> 00:22:30.480
if you walk the walk.

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:36.210
So if the United States is doing what it needs
to do at home, but also producing and making

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:42.039
available a percentage of what it is we generate
here in the way of vaccines, it makes it so

00:22:42.039 --> 00:22:46.470
much easier for this government to go to other
governments, hey, follow our lead.

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:47.470
SIERRA: Right.

00:22:47.470 --> 00:22:51.390
HAASS: Otherwise, it's called hypocrisy, and
hypocrisy doesn't do you any good in foreign

00:22:51.390 --> 00:22:54.330
policy any more than it does you good in your
day-to-day life.

00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:58.930
But if we're acting consistently, and we're
not asking others to do more than we ourselves

00:22:58.930 --> 00:23:03.010
are prepared to do, then I think we've got
a very strong hand.

00:23:03.010 --> 00:23:06.320
SIERRA: What is your definition of success?

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:08.190
When it comes to COVID?

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:09.960
What would life look like?

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:11.430
What will it take to get there?

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:14.950
You know, and besides vaccines, what else
is required?

00:23:14.950 --> 00:23:20.150
FAUCI: Success for me, as a medical doctor,
a physician, and a scientist, at a public

00:23:20.150 --> 00:23:23.120
health service, is the elimination of COVID-19.

00:23:23.120 --> 00:23:28.821
Namely, where there are so few cases in the
country where you don't even notice it, there

00:23:28.821 --> 00:23:30.480
are so few.

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:34.720
And that you get to by a successful vaccination
program.

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:39.620
And as we've been discussing, you want to
make sure it stays that way, by vaccinating

00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:42.919
the entire world and getting the entire world
protected.

00:23:42.919 --> 00:23:48.380
SIERRA: Well, this was wonderful, hopefully
someday very soon I'll be able to run into

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:53.840
you at, you know, L&amp;B Spumoni Gardens or somewhere,
and things will be right back, sir.

00:23:53.840 --> 00:23:56.090
FAUCI: Yep, I hope so.

00:23:56.090 --> 00:23:58.059
Without masks.

00:23:58.059 --> 00:24:04.370
SIERRA: As we move through the tunnel and
out towards the light, we have to remember

00:24:04.370 --> 00:24:07.419
that there are a lot of people moving along
with us.

00:24:07.419 --> 00:24:10.900
More than seven billion people, in fact.

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:15.059
If we’re going to make it to the end, we
have to get there together.

00:24:15.059 --> 00:24:19.570
The first lesson of COVID was that what happens
somewhere far away can change every part of

00:24:19.570 --> 00:24:20.799
your life at home.

00:24:20.799 --> 00:24:23.970
It may turn out to be the last lesson too.

00:24:23.970 --> 00:24:28.950
Helping your neighbors, whether a state over,
a country over, or an ocean over, isn’t

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:38.520
just the right thing, it’s the smart thing.

